"downpour", "gale"]}, {"species": "croakle", "min": 95, "max": 97, "weight": 10, "time": ["dawn", "day", "dusk"]}, {"species": "gannetling", "min": 95, "max": 97, "weight": 10}, {"species": "swiftlet", "min": 95, "max": 97, "weight": 8, "weather": ["gale", "clear"]}], "water": [{"species": "raylette", "min": 95, "max": 97, "weight": 28}, {"species": "dolphlet", "min": 95, "max": 97, "weight": 22}, {"species": "pinchkin", "min": 95, "max": 97, "weight": 16}, {"species": "plungebeak", "min": 95, "max": 97, "weight": 12}, {"species": "sandbarray", "min": 96, "max": 98, "weight": 6}, {"species": "swelklet", "min": 95, "max": 97, "weight": 16}]}, "signs": [{"x": 32, "y": 34, "text": "GEORGE TOWN, GRAND CAYMAN. Capital, harbour and, on a cruise day, a queue. Pedestrians have right of way. Chickens have a claim."}, {"x": 41, "y": 25, "text": "STINGRAY CITY TOURS. Boats to the sandbar every hour. Please do not pick up the stingrays. They have feelings. Mostly about squid."}, {"x": 15, "y": 26, "text": "WEST BAY, with Hell. Postcards, T-shirts and the most honest advertising on the island."}, {"x": 56, "y": 37, "text": "THE QUEEN ELIZABETH II BOTANIC PARK. Blue iguanas, the Mastic Trail and a lot of orchids. Look, don't lick."}, {"x": 25, "y": 38, "text": "THE WRECK OF THE TEN SAIL. In 1794 a convoy of ships ran onto the reef off East End one night. Caymanians rowed out and saved most of the crews. The story goes that King George III rewarded the islands with freedom from taxes and conscription. Historians have never found the royal document, but the islands have never had an income tax.", "plaque": "cayman_ten_sail", "title": "The Wreck of the Ten Sail"}, {"x": 13, "y": 34, "text": "THE ISLANDS OF TURTLES. Columbus sighted the Brac and Little Cayman in 1503 and called them Las Tortugas, after the turtles. Later maps said Caymanas, from a word for crocodile. Green turtles were hunted so hard that Caymanian turtlers had to sail to Nicaraguan waters to find them.", "plaque": "cayman_turtles", "title": "The Islands of Turtles"}, {"x": 12, "y": 26, "text": "SEVEN MILE BEACH. It is about five and a half miles long. Nobody minds. It is one of the great beaches of the Caribbean, with white coral sand and the sort of sunsets people hold up their phones for.", "plaque": "cayman_seven_mile", "title": "Seven Mile Beach"}, {"x": 40, "y": 21, "text": "STINGRAY CITY. Southern stingrays began gathering on a sandbar in the North Sound when fishermen cleaned their catch there. Today they come for squid from visitors and swim round your legs like friendly doormats. The water is about waist deep, which is ideal for standing very still.", "plaque": "cayman_stingray", "title": "Stingray City"}, {"x": 64, "y": 35, "text": "THE BLUE IGUANA. The blue iguana lives only on Grand Cayman. By the early 2000s only a handful survived in the wild, hit by cars, dogs and loss of habitat. A breeding programme in the Botanic Park has raised and released hundreds, and the wild population is now over a thousand.", "plaque": "cayman_blue_iguana", "title": "The Blue Iguana"}, {"x": 50, "y": 35, "text": "PEDRO ST. JAMES. Built around 1780, this is the oldest surviving stone building in the Cayman Islands. In 1831 islanders met here to set up a local government, which is why it's called the birthplace of Cayman's democracy.", "plaque": "cayman_pedro", "title": "Pedro St. James"}, {"x": 15, "y": 20, "text": "HELL. A patch of jagged black limestone in West Bay. Visitors can buy a postcard and post it from Hell. The black comes from algae and fungus growing on the rock, not from anything infernal, though the shop does not mention this.", "plaque": "cayman_hell", "title": "Hell"}, {"x": 60, "y": 38, "text": "CAYMAN BRAC. Brac is Gaelic for bluff, and the island has a 140-foot limestone Bluff to prove it. It lies north-east of Grand Cayman, a short flight away, along with Little Cayman, home to about a hundred and fifty people and a great many iguanas.", "plaque": "cayman_brac", "title": "Cayman Brac"}, {"x": 46, "y": 28, "text": "THE CAYMAN PARROT. The Grand Cayman parrot is green with a white forehead and a flash of red, and is the national bird. They nest in tree holes and are extremely loud about it. A flock sounds like an argument at a very small airport.", "plaque": "cayman_parrot", "title": "The Cayman Parrot"}, {"x": 17, "y": 28, "text": "PIRATES WEEK. Every November George Town is invaded by pirates on purpose. There is a mock capture of the town, a parade and fireworks, and a lot of plastic cutlasses. It has run since 1977 and the pirates have never yet won.", "plaque": "cayman_pirates_week", "title": "Pirates Week"}, {"x": 22, "y": 37, "text": "FORT GEORGE. Only a wall remains of Fort George, built in the 1790s to guard George Town against raiders from the sea. The cannon pointing at the water have never been fired in anger, which is the best thing a cannon can say.", "plaque": "cayman_fort_george", "title": "Fort George"}, {"x": 38, "y": 28, "text": "THE CAYMAN DOLLAR. The Cayman Islands dollar is pegged at about one dollar to 1.20 US dollars, which makes it worth more than the American one. Banks and law firms in George Town handle business from all over the world, and every office has a view of the sea.", "plaque": "cayman_dollar", "title": "The Cayman Dollar"}], "legend_extra": {"_water": {"deep": "#0a5a82", "deep_top": "#38b6cc", "shallow": "#46d6cc"}, "$": {"tile": "sea", "colour": "#12809c"}, "~": {"tile": "water", "colour": "#33b0bd"}, "w": {"tile": "shallow", "colour": "#7fe0d2"}, ".": {"tile": "grass", "colour": "#74ad3f"}, ",": {"tile": "tall_grass", "colour": "#5e9a3a", "prop": "short"}, "s": {"tile": "sand", "colour": "#f4ead0"}, "p": {"tile": "sand", "colour": "#f6efd9"}, "t": {"tile": "tree_oak", "prop": "palm"}, "e": {"tile": "tree_oak", "prop": "palm"}, "r": {"tile": "rock", "colour": "#8c8678"}, "k": {"tile": "rock", "colour": "#2b2b2e", "prop": "gritstone"}, "#": {"tile": "path", "colour": "#e4d6b4"}, "=": {"tile